  "voice_id": "q0IMILNRPxOgtBTS4taI",
  "model_id": "eleven_v4_turbo",
  "text": "In C, every variable is a box in memory... and every box has an address. Here, n holds forty-two. And ampersand n? That's the address of that box. [curious] So what's a pointer? It's just a variable that holds an address. Int star p, set to ampersand n, stores where n lives. Star p goes to that address. Reading star p gives you forty-two, and writing one hundred through it changes n ITSELF. On this machine, an int takes four bytes, and a pointer takes eight. The addresses change on every run, but p always holds the address of n.\n\nNow, here's the classic trap. This swap takes two ints and trades them. But C passes copies. So swap gets two new boxes, a and b, holding one and two. The copies trade... and x and y? They stay exactly as they were.\n\nSo, pass the addresses instead. Call swap with ampersand x and ampersand y, and a and b now hold where x and y live. Now star a and star b reach back into main's boxes, so x and y REALLY trade.\n\nAnd that's all a pointer is. Ampersand asks where a variable lives, and star goes there.",
